 and Nb, and this affects recrystallization retardation and strain-induced precipitation potential. V precipitation also proceeds during holding at coiling temperature. [Data: Entities (123, 122, 474, 482, 481); Relationships (153, 858, 861, 810, 881)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Strength governed by multiple hardening mechanisms\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Strength in controlled-rolled steel is greater than in conventionally hot-rolled or normalized steel at the same carbon equivalent. Improvements come from rolling passes, controlled rolling, grain refinement, precipitation hardening, solid-solution hardening, transformation hardening, and alloying elements, notably Nb and/or V, and Mo. Nb additions increase strength, even small additions cause large increases, but small Nb addition can reduce notch toughness in as-hot-rolled plates. Decreasing T_KI by every 10 C increases strength by 10-15 N/mm2 in low-pearlite niobium steels and reduces Nb or V consumption. [Data: Entities (6, 102, 907, 916, 1291); Relationships (115, 125, 1862, 1847, 2789, +more)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Grain refinement improves both strength and toughness\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Grain refinement is a long-known advantage in steel rolling and is used by controlled rolling. Heavy repeated deformation refines both austenite and ferrite, contributing to increased strength and toughness. It can be attained by static recrystallization, fine dispersion of a second phase, or lowering the transformation temperature with substitutional alloying elements. Repeated recrystallization reaches a limiting value and can leave relatively coarse alpha grains; deformation in the non-recrystallization region reaches a limiting value at 60-70% reduction, while two-phase deformation causes further refinement. Grain refinement is exceptional in reducing transition temperature while raising yield strength. [Data: Entities (55, 902, 908, 905, 887); Relationships (51, 1777, 1847, 1860, 1776, +more)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Toughness and transition temperature trade-offs\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Toughness is improved by rolling passes, controlled-rolling routes, grain refinement, fine ferrite grain size, Nb additions, and finish rolling at lower temperatures. Finish-rolling temperature affects gamma grain size; lowering it improves toughness, and inadequate control can form coarse polygonal ferrite. Heavy deformation in the two-phase/intercritical region also improves toughness, though reductions in the intercritical interval can decrease ductility indices. Controlled-rolled steel can achieve very low longitudinal and transverse transition temperatures by sacrificing through-thickness toughness. Coarse grain structures, upper bainite, coarse ferrite, and Ti addition decrease toughness. [Data: Entities (7, 28, 67, 1205, 1204); Relationships (122, 1334, 2616, 2656, 1908, +more)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Controlled-rolled steel microstructure and anisotropy\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Controlled-rolled steel often is microalloyed with Nb, V, and Ti, with Nb and Ti considered indispensable. Ferrite nucleation occurs in grain interiors as well as at grain boundaries, leading to a refined grain structure and crystallographic texture. It can exhibit a mixed microstructure of fine and coarse alpha grains and/or upper bainite, and texture development can cause anisotropy, plane anisotropy, through-thickness embrittlement, and separations. Low-temperature toughness can decrease when mixed fine and coarse alpha grains and/or upper bainite are present. [Data: Entities (28, 29, 33, 1188, 275); Relationships (25, 29, 2565, 433, 2323)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Precipitation during cooling and coiling\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Precipitation hardening is a strengthening mechanism operating through precipitates. In early development, Nb was employed mainly for precipitation hardening, and the strengthening results from fine Nb(C,N) precipitates formed in the alpha matrix during cooling. Strain-induced precipitates such as Nb(C,N) prevent alpha-grain growth during gamma-to-alpha transformation. Holding at coiling temperature causes precipitation of Nb and V and a large increase in yield strength, and the electrolytic-extraction technique is used to determine precipitation progress. [Data: Entities (102, 919, 1033, 1029, 827); Relationships (125, 1914, 2214, 2202, 2158, +more)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Alloying elements and weldability\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Cu and Ni are added in small amounts to controlled-rolled steel, have little effect on properties, and are effective with Nb/V. Ti is indispensable in controlled-rolled steel but decreases toughness in the base steel and heat-affected zone and can slow grain growth. Carbon equivalent affects weldability, which improves as carbon equivalent and carbon content decrease. Pearlite-free steel was proposed to improve weldability and toughness by reducing C, increasing strength with Nb and/or V, and improving toughness by low finishing temperature. [Data: Entities (947, 657, 886, 629, 136); Relationships (1960, 1303, 1815, 1206, 147, +more)]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 7.5,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"The community is highly significant because Nb microalloying and controlled rolling underpin strength-toughness balance, weldability, and industrial high-strength steel design.\"\n}"}, "metrics": {"attempted_request_count": 1, "successful_response_count": 0, "failed_response_count": 0, "responses_with_tokens": 1, "prompt_tokens": 10147, "completion_tokens": 6666, "total_tokens": 16813, "compute_duration_seconds": 50.36036014556885, "streaming_responses": 0, "retries": 0, "requests_with_retries": 0}}}